TI  - STEADY-STATE ENERGETICS OF A PLANKTONIC HERBIVORE
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE MARINE BIOLOGICAL ASSOCIATION OF THE UNITED KINGDOM
LA  - English
AB  - The marine rotifer Brachionus plicatilis Muller was grown bacteria-free in a chemostat with the alga Brachiomonas submarina, labeled with 57Co vitamin B12 as the limiting nutrient. State variables were recorded at a number of steady states between zero dilution rate and washout. These were algal input biomass, algal (and fecal) output biomass, rotifer (and gut) output biomass, in terms of C, N and vitamin B12; and input and output dissolved vitamin B12. From these were calculated input, ingestion, assimilation, excretion and growth rates and the associated transfer efficiencies. Ingestion and assimilation efficiencies were high and not obviously growth rate dependent; the growth efficiency was interpreted as ranging from zero at zero growth rate to a maximum at half maximal growth and back to zero at washout, being the result of the C and N excretion rates, which, finite at zero growth rate, increased with increasing rapidity with increasing growth rate, tending to infinity as the maximum growth rate was approached. N and C excretion rates were correlated. Slow-adapted and fast-adapted modes of growth were recognized with characteristically different growth and energy parameters. The reasons for and implications of these findings are discussed.
