TI  - THE VITAMIN-B12 REQUIREMENT OF THE MARINE ROTIFER BRACHIONUS-PLICATILIS
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE MARINE BIOLOGICAL ASSOCIATION OF THE UNITED KINGDOM
LA  - English
AB  - The marine rotifer B. plicatilis has a definitive vitamin B12 requirement. The vitamin is taken up directly from solution and not from the food-algae. The rate of uptake by the rotifer was 0.1 pg/animal per day. Vitamin B12 may not be atypical of water soluble micronutrients and many of these nutrients may be taken up in this way by the rotifer. B12 excreted by the alga Dunaliella was a source of the vitamin for the animal, but intracellular cobalamins are not available. The vitamin B12 uptake rates by the food-algae Brachiomonas and Dunaliella were determined and the saturation level measured for the latter. This alga, which is a non-B12 requirer synthesized sufficient vitamin to support rotifer growth in the absence of external free vitamin B12.
