TI  - URBAN GROUNDWATER AS A POSSIBLE NUTRIENT SOURCE FOR AN ESTUARINE BENTHIC ALGAL BLOOM
T2  - ESTUARINE COASTAL AND SHELF SCIENCE
LA  - English
AB  - The Peel Inlet in Western Australia was used to study the cause of eutrophic conditions in an estuary. In addition to large quantities of N and P (at low concentrations) entering the inlet from rivers and drains from agricultural areas, the urban contribution via groundwater was identified. The average N concentration of urban groundwater under an area serviced with septic tank systems was 12 ppm. Because of the nature of the soils, very few groundwater samples contained appreciable P concentrations. One area close to the inlet had semiconfined groundwaters with N and P concentrations as high as 100 and 0.4 ppm, respectively. An obsolete dumping site for human excreta was also identified near the urban area, and this is suspected of having contributed to very high concentrations of N and P in groundwater which may have already reached the estuary and caused excessive algal growth. Groundwater contours showed the potential for groundwater movement from part of the urban area and also from the obsolete dumping site.
