AB  - Undescribed mussels (seep mytilid la) harboring methanotrophic endosymbionts exhibit high biomass around hydrocarbon seeps on the Louisiana Slope of the Gulf of Mexico. These mussels assimilate ammonium and nitrate present at high concentrations in their environment. Pathways of assimilation were investigated by enzyme activity measurements and N-15 tracer experiments. Glutamine synthetase was detected in all freshly collected mussels tested. Nitrate reductase activity was not always observed. Exposure to (NH3)-N-15 resulted in the appearance of millimolar concentrations of (NH3)-N-15 within the symbiont-containing tissues. The concentration of internal (NH3)-N-15 was several times higher than in the medium and correlated with (NH3)-N-15 assimilation rate. These results indicate that exogenous (NH3)-N-15 was taken up into a large internal pool before it was assimilated. Our results do not indicate the extent to which ammonium pools were within the host or symbiont or whether ammonium assimilation was facilitated by either partner exclusively. The observation of elevated internal ammonium concentrations is inconsistent with the ''depletion-diffusion'' mechanism of nutrient uptake proposed for algal-invertebrate symbioses and is suggestive of active ammonium uptake mechanisms across the host surface. Exposure to (NO3-)-N-15 also resulted in the appearance of (NH3)-N-15. With internal (NH3)-N-15 concentration correlated with (NO3-)-N-15 assimilation rate. This result indicates that (NO3-)-N-15 was reduced more rapidly than it was assimilated and that (NH3)-N-15 derived from (NO3-)-N-15 may also enter an internal ammonium pool. Assimilation of nitrate in the presence of millimolar concentrations of internal ammonium and reduction of (NO3-)-N-15 in excess of assimilation is consistent with the functioning of dissimilatory nitrate reduction pathways with ammonium as a major endproduct. Such a mechanism may operate in other chemosynthetic symbioses that exhibit dissimilatory nitrate reduction.
